::ENG4_U02_L07_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.W.01] Passage: A writer plans a story in which an older sister wants to leave her town, while her younger brother sees leaving as abandonment. Which plan best supports a complex narrative? {
~A. List every place in the town without showing conflict.#A setting list does not create narrative complexity.
=B. Use a scene where both siblings interpret the same departure differently.#Correct. Shared events with different interpretations support complexity.
~C. Explain in the first sentence that leaving is bad.#This states a lesson too directly.
~D. Avoid character motivation so the plot moves quickly.#Motivation is necessary for perspective and theme.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.03] Passage: Lina saves the train ticket because it represents freedom. Tomas hides the same ticket because it represents losing his sister. What does the ticket reveal? {
~A. Both characters have identical motivations.#Their motivations contrast.
~B. The story has no conflict.#The object reveals conflict.
~C. Objects cannot develop perspective.#The ticket develops perspective.
=D. The same object can carry different meanings for different perspectives.#Correct. Lina and Tomas attach different emotional meanings to it.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Passage: A character realizes that protecting another person from pain can sometimes prevent that person from growing. Which universal theme does this suggest? {
=A. Care can become harmful when it prevents necessary growth.#Correct. This expresses a universal theme with complexity.
~B. Growth never requires discomfort.#This contradicts the passage.
~C. Protection is always wrong.#This is too absolute.
~D. People should never help each other.#This is unsupported.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Passage: Weak draft: The argument was intense and important. Which revision best shows rather than tells? {
~A. The argument was very intense and very important.#This only adds repetition.
~B. The argument had significance and relevance.#This is abstract.
=C. Mina folded the letter until its corners split, but she still would not look at her father.#Correct. The action reveals tension.
~D. The argument happened, and people talked.#This is vague.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: The writer wants a correct sentence. Which sentence is correct? {
~A. When the door closed Selene understood, why her brother had stayed silent.#The comma placement is incorrect.
~B. When the door closed, Selene understand why her brother had stayed silent.#The verb does not agree.
=C. When the door closed, Selene understood why her brother had stayed silent.#Correct. The dependent clause is followed by a comma.
~D. when the door closed, Selene understood why her brother had stayed silent.#The sentence should begin with a capital letter.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.W.01] Passage: A narrative about loyalty begins with a character defending a friend, then discovering the friend harmed someone else. Which choice best deepens the narrative? {
=A. Show the character struggling between loyalty to the friend and responsibility to the harmed person.#Correct. This creates meaningful internal conflict.
~B. End immediately after the discovery with no response.#This prevents development.
~C. Add an unrelated description of the weather only.#Unrelated description does not deepen conflict.
~D. Remove the character's motivation.#Motivation is needed for complex narrative.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.R.03] Passage: "I kept the medal in a drawer," Nia says, "because everyone called it courage, but I remembered only fear." What perspective is revealed? {
~A. Nia sees the medal as simple proof of happiness.#This contradicts her memory of fear.
~B. Nia does not remember the event at all.#She remembers fear.
~C. Nia wants everyone to praise her more.#There is no evidence for that.
=D. Nia has a conflicted perspective on public praise and private memory.#Correct. The medal means more than public courage to her.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Passage: A character leaves home to gain independence but later understands that independence also requires responsibility to others. Which theme is most precise? {
~A. Leaving home is always selfish.#This is too absolute.
=B. True independence includes responsibility, not just escape.#Correct. This captures the complexity of the theme.
~C. Independence means never needing anyone.#This contradicts the responsibility element.
~D. Home is only a physical place.#This misses the responsibility idea.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Passage: Original sentence: The room was sad. Which revision best improves precision and narrative effect? {
~A. The room was sad and gloomy and not happy.#This is repetitive.
~B. The room had a room feeling.#This is vague.
=C. The unopened curtains left the room gray, and the dust on the piano keys looked untouched by any living hand.#Correct. Concrete details create mood.
~D. The room was emotionally relevant.#This is abstract.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Choose the sentence with correct punctuation. {
=A. I wanted to speak, but the words stayed behind my teeth.#Correct. The comma before but joins two independent clauses.
~B. I wanted to speak but, the words stayed behind my teeth.#The comma is misplaced.
~C. I wanted, to speak but the words stayed behind my teeth.#The comma after wanted is incorrect.
~D. I wanted to speak but the words, stayed behind my teeth.#The comma before stayed is incorrect.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.W.01] Passage: A student writes a narrative in which every paragraph explains the moral directly. What is the main weakness? {
~A. The narrative has too much conflict.#Direct moral explanation is the issue, not conflict.
~B. The narrative uses too many characters.#The passage does not say this.
~C. The narrative has no words.#It has paragraphs.
=D. The narrative may tell the theme instead of developing it through action and perspective.#Correct. Theme should emerge through narrative craft.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.R.03] Passage: Theo tells his mother he is leaving because the town is too small, but privately he fears he will never become anyone important if he stays. What does the private thought reveal? {
~A. Theo's stated reason and deeper motivation are exactly the same.#The private thought adds a deeper motive.
=B. Theo's perspective includes ambition and insecurity beneath his explanation.#Correct. The private thought complicates his perspective.
~C. Theo has no reason for leaving.#He has several reasons.
~D. Theo's mother controls the entire story.#No evidence supports this.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Passage: A story shows that a family argument ends only after each person admits a partial truth. Which theme is best supported? {
~A. Arguments are always solved by silence.#The passage emphasizes admission, not silence.
=B. Understanding often begins when people recognize that their own view is incomplete.#Correct. The theme connects admission to understanding.
~C. Families should never disagree.#This is unrealistic and unsupported.
~D. Truth has no role in relationships.#Truth is central.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Passage: A peer says, "Your scene has a clear conflict, but I cannot tell what the narrator values." Which revision target best addresses the feedback? {
~A. Add more random background information.#Random background may not reveal values.
~B. Remove all dialogue.#Dialogue may help reveal values.
=C. Add an action or thought that shows what the narrator is afraid to lose.#Correct. This reveals values through perspective.
~D. Change every sentence to passive voice.#This does not address the feedback.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Which sentence maintains consistent verb tense? {
=A. She opened the letter and realized the truth.#Correct. Both verbs are past tense.
~B. She opened the letter and realizes the truth.#The tense shifts from past to present.
~C. She opens the letter and realized the truth.#The tense shifts from present to past.
~D. She opening the letter and realized the truth.#This is not a complete correct verb pattern.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.W.01] Passage: A narrative scene includes a cracked watch that belonged to a character's father. How can the writer best use the object? {
~A. Mention it once with no connection to character or theme.#This would limit its function.
~B. Replace all dialogue with a description of the watch parts.#That would likely overload the scene.
~C. Use it only to show the exact time.#This is too literal.
=D. Use it to reveal the character's relationship to memory, time, and loss.#Correct. The object can carry symbolic and emotional meaning.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.R.03] Passage: In a scene, Jalen laughs while everyone else is silent, but his hands shake under the table. What does this contrast reveal? {
~A. Jalen is completely relaxed.#His shaking hands suggest otherwise.
~B. Jalen has no perspective.#His actions reveal perspective.
=C. Jalen's outward behavior hides inner fear or tension.#Correct. The contrast reveals conflicting layers of perspective.
~D. Everyone else is joyful.#Everyone else is silent.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Passage: A character wins a public award but feels ashamed because the victory required betraying a friend. Which theme is most complex? {
=A. Success can be morally complicated when it costs a relationship.#Correct. This captures the conflict.
~B. Winning is always good.#This ignores shame and betrayal.
~C. Friends are irrelevant to success.#The friend matters.
~D. Awards have no meaning in stories.#The award has thematic function.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Passage: A draft scene contains three pages of backstory before the conflict appears. What revision would most improve pacing? {
~A. Add even more backstory before the conflict.#This worsens pacing.
=B. Begin closer to the moment when the character must choose.#Correct. Starting near conflict improves pacing.
~C. Remove the conflict entirely.#Conflict is needed.
~D. Replace the scene with a vocabulary list.#This is not narrative writing.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Which sentence is clearest and most correct? {
~A. The letters on the table, which were unopened.#This is a fragment.
~B. The unopened letters waiting on the table while Ana searched for courage.#This is a fragment.
~C. The unopened letters, while Ana searched for courage.#This is a fragment.
=D. The unopened letters waited on the table while Ana searched for courage.#Correct. The sentence is complete and clear.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.W.01] Passage: A student wants to write about forgiveness. Which conflict best supports a universal theme about forgiveness? {
=A. A character must decide whether forgiveness means restoring trust or simply releasing anger.#Correct. This creates a complex theme.
~B. A character forgives someone before any harm occurs.#No conflict exists.
~C. A character forgets the entire event immediately.#Forgetting is not the same as forgiveness.
~D. A character has no relationship with anyone else.#Forgiveness needs relational context.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.R.03] Passage: Priya says she wants everyone to be honest, but she hides the invitation because she fears being excluded. What does this reveal? {
~A. Priya has no motive.#Fear is a motive.
~B. Priya values honesty without conflict.#Her action conflicts with the value.
=C. Priya's perspective is conflicted because her stated value clashes with her fear.#Correct. Her action complicates her stated belief.
~D. Priya is not part of the story.#She is central to the described action.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Passage: A narrative ends with the character returning the key but keeping the memory of why it mattered. Which theme is most likely? {
~A. Objects matter only if they are expensive.#The key's value is symbolic, not financial.
~B. Returning objects erases the past.#The memory remains.
~C. Keys are always the most important symbols.#This is too broad.
=D. Memory can remain even after a person releases a physical object.#Correct. The ending separates object from memory.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Passage: A teacher comments, "Your theme is clear, but the ending explains too much." Which revision is best? {
~A. Add a long paragraph explaining the theme again.#This worsens the problem.
=B. Replace the explanation with a final image or action that lets the reader infer the theme.#Correct. This shows theme through craft.
~C. Remove all evidence of theme.#The theme still needs development.
~D. Add unrelated dialogue.#Unrelated dialogue does not solve the issue.
}

::ENG4_U02_L07_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Which sentence uses pronouns clearly? {
=A. When Elena handed Ruth the journal, Elena said she was ready to tell the truth.#Correct. Repeating Elena avoids confusion.
~B. When Elena handed Ruth the journal, she said she was ready to tell the truth.#The pronoun she could refer to Elena or Ruth.
~C. When she handed her the journal, she said she was ready.#The pronouns are unclear.
~D. She handed her it because she said it was time.#The pronouns are unclear.
}
